      "output": "**Recommendation:** Pursue an internal move to a data\u2011science\u2011focused role at your current company while simultaneously building a portfolio of end\u2011to\u2011end ML projects.\n\n**Trade\u2011off:** This path lets you leverage existing relationships and domain knowledge (low risk, faster ramp\u2011up) but may limit exposure to a broader tech stack if your firm\u2019s data\u2011science practice is narrow. An external job would give fresh tools and networks but requires a full job search and risk of relocation constraints.\n\n**Next actions**\n\n1. **Map the internal path** \u2013\u202fIdentify any open \u201cData Scientist\u202f/\u202fMachine Learning Engineer\u201d positions or \u201cData Analyst \u2192 Data Scientist\u201d transition programs within your Berlin office; set up an informational chat with the hiring manager or a senior data scientist this week.  \n2. **Showcase transferable work** \u2013\u202fSelect two recent analyst projects where you applied statistical modelling or predictive analytics; rewrite them as case studies (problem, data, method, impact) and add them to a GitHub repo with clear notebooks and a short README.  \n3. **Close the skill gap** \u2013\u202fEnroll in a focused, project\u2011based ML course (e.g., Coursera\u2019s \u201cMachine Learning Engineering for Production\u201d or a local bootcamp) and commit to completing one capstone that mirrors a business problem you\u2019ve seen at work within the next 8\u202fweeks.  \n\nThese steps keep you rooted in Berlin, build the concrete evidence hiring managers need, and position you to negotiate a data\u2011science title internally before you consider external offers. Let me know if you need help structuring the case\u2011study write\u2011up or selecting a suitable ML course.",
