      "output": "**Assumption:**\u202fYour dissertation will involve a literature review, data collection, and analysis typical for an urban\u2011economics PhD project, and you have roughly\u202f12\u202fweeks to complete the reading\u2011and\u2011analysis phase before writing.\n\n### Week\u2011by\u2011Week Reading & Analysis Schedule  \n\n| Week | Goal | Core Activities (\u2248\u202f10\u202fh) |\n|------|------|--------------------------|\n| 1 | Scope & framing |\u202fDefine research question; list 5\u20117 key sub\u2011questions; compile a preliminary bibliography of 15\u202fseminal works (search terms: \u201curban agglomeration economies\u201d, \u201cspatial equilibrium\u201d, \u201ccity size distribution\u201d). |\n| 2 | Theoretical foundations |\u202fRead 3\u202ffoundational books/monographs; write a 1\u2011page summary of each theory\u2019s assumptions and predictions; note gaps relevant to your sub\u2011questions. |\n| 3 | Methodological landscape |\u202fSurvey 8\u202fmethodology papers (e.g., spatial econometrics, GIS\u2011based regressions); create a comparison table of data requirements, strengths, and limitations. |\n| 4 | Empirical benchmarks I |\u202fIdentify 4\u202frecent empirical studies on your core topic; extract their data sources, model specifications, and key findings; flag any contradictory results. |\n| 5 | Empirical benchmarks II |\u202fRepeat for another 4\u202fstudies focusing on different geographic contexts or time periods; begin a synthesis matrix linking each study to your sub\u2011questions. |\n| 6 | Data\u2011source audit |\u202fCatalogue all potential datasets (census, land\u2011use, transport, housing price indices); assess accessibility, granularity, and licensing; shortlist 2\u20113 primary sources. |\n| 7 | Critical gaps |\u202fUsing the synthesis matrix, pinpoint where existing literature leaves unanswered questions; draft a \u201cresearch gap\u201d paragraph to anchor your contribution. |\n| 8 | Preliminary model design |\u202fSketch candidate empirical models (specify dependent/independent variables, fixed effects, spatial lags); note required variables and any proxy measures. |\n| 9 | Feasibility check |\u202fMatch model requirements to shortlisted datasets; identify missing variables or measurement issues; plan supplemental data collection if needed. |\n| 10 | Refine literature narrative |\u202fWrite a 2\u2011page \u201cstate of the art\u201d draft integrating theory, methods, and empirical findings; highlight how your model addresses the identified gaps. |\n| 11 | Peer feedback |\u202fShare the draft with advisor or a colleague; collect comments on clarity, relevance, and methodological soundness. |\n| 12 | Revise & finalize reading log |\u202fIncorporate feedback; produce a concise reading log (citation, key contribution, relevance to each sub\u2011question). This log will feed directly into your dissertation chapter. |\n\n**Next step:**\u202fConfirm the overall timeline (e.g., total weeks available) and any specific data or methodological constraints you already know, so I can adjust the schedule accordingly.",
